import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.NpvCl_7q.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"How it works","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/how-it-works.md","filePath":"guide/how-it-works.md","lastUpdated":1792608207000}'),s={name:"guide/how-it-works.md"};function n(r,e,l,d,c,h){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="how-it-works" tabindex="-1">How it works <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-it-works" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How it works&quot;">​</a></h1><p>You do not need any of this to use MasonKit, but a rough picture of the moving parts makes layout behaviour easier to predict.</p><h2 id="three-layers" tabindex="-1">Three layers <a class="header-anchor" href="#three-layers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Three layers&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>TypeScript</strong> (<code>@triniwiz/nativescript-masonkit</code>)</td><td>Element classes (<code>Div</code>, <code>P</code>, <code>View</code>, <code>Img</code>, ...) and the CSS properties MasonKit registers with NativeScript&#39;s styling system.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Native views</strong> (Swift on iOS, Kotlin on Android, C++/WinRT on Windows)</td><td>One native view per element. Draws backgrounds, borders, shadows and text, handles touch, scrolling and accessibility.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mason core</strong> (Rust, built on <a href="https://github.com/DioxusLabs/taffy" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">taffy</a>)</td><td>Holds the layout tree and computes every box&#39;s size and position: flexbox, grid, block and inline flow, floats, positioning.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The same Rust core runs on all three platforms, which is why one stylesheet lays out the same way on each of them.</p><h2 id="from-css-to-pixels" tabindex="-1">From CSS to pixels <a class="header-anchor" href="#from-css-to-pixels" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;From CSS to pixels&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>NativeScript matches your CSS.</strong> Selectors, classes, specificity, <code>@media</code> and <code>var()</code> are handled by NativeScript&#39;s own CSS engine, exactly as for any other view.</li><li><strong>MasonKit receives each declaration.</strong> Its property handlers parse the value with web semantics (CSS <code>px</code>, <code>rem</code>, <code>em</code>, <code>calc()</code>, ...) and write it into the node&#39;s style. The style lives in memory shared by the JS and native sides, so setting a property does not cost a native call per value. Changes made in the same turn of the event loop are flushed together.</li><li><strong>Mason computes layout.</strong> When the tree is dirty, the Rust core lays it out in one pass against the space available.</li><li><strong>Native views are placed.</strong> Each view gets its computed frame and repaints if needed.</li></ol><p>Reading a layout-dependent value such as <code>scrollHeight</code> or <code>clientWidth</code> runs any pending layout first, as it does in a browser.</p><h2 id="mason-trees-and-roots" tabindex="-1">Mason trees and roots <a class="header-anchor" href="#mason-trees-and-roots" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Mason trees and roots&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A MasonKit element whose parent is <strong>not</strong> a MasonKit element (a <code>Page</code>, <code>GridLayout</code>, <code>StackLayout</code>, ...) is the <strong>root</strong> of a Mason tree:</p><ul><li>It is measured by NativeScript like any other view. When NativeScript gives it a definite size, the root lays out against that size. When the space is unconstrained, it sizes to its max-content size.</li><li>Every MasonKit descendant is then placed by the Mason core in the same pass, without going back through NativeScript&#39;s measure and layout for each view.</li></ul><p>A MasonKit element nested inside another one is just a node in its parent&#39;s tree.</p><p>A plain NativeScript view inside a MasonKit element (for example a <code>Label</code> or a <code>ListView</code> inside a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>) becomes a single <strong>leaf</strong> box. Mason asks NativeScript to measure it, places it with flex or grid like any other child, and NativeScript lays out whatever is inside it. See <a href="/nativescript-mason/guide/mixing-views">Mixing with NativeScript views</a>.</p><h2 id="elements-are-native-views" tabindex="-1">Elements are native views <a class="header-anchor" href="#elements-are-native-views" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Elements are native views&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every element you create is a real native view: a <code>UIView</code> subclass on iOS, an Android <code>View</code> or <code>ViewGroup</code>, a XAML element on Windows. There is no web view and no HTML engine involved.</p><h3 id="text" tabindex="-1">Text <a class="header-anchor" href="#text" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Text&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A text element (<code>&lt;p&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>, ...) is a single native text view. Inline children inside it, such as a <code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> inside a <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>, become styled runs of that one text view rather than separate views, so a paragraph with ten styled words is still one view. Taps on a run are still delivered to the element that produced it. Inline elements placed directly in a block such as a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> are grouped the same way: consecutive inline children share one anonymous text container. Non-text inline content, such as an <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> or an <code>inline-block</code> element on the line, stays a real view positioned within the text.</p><p>Each platform shapes and measures text with its native text stack (CoreText on iOS, Android&#39;s text layout APIs, DirectWrite on Windows), so fonts, glyphs and line breaking look native. Mason uses those measurements to size text boxes during layout.</p><h3 id="block-containers-can-scroll" tabindex="-1">Block containers can scroll <a class="header-anchor" href="#block-containers-can-scroll" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Block containers can scroll&quot;">​</a></h3><p>As in a browser, every block box can become a scroll container. <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;section&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;article&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> and the other block elements all scroll when you give them <code>overflow: auto</code> or <code>overflow: scroll</code> and their content is larger than their box. With the default <code>overflow: visible</code> they neither scroll nor clip.</p><p>Scrolling is implemented by MasonKit itself rather than the system scroll view, so nesting scroll containers inside each other works. See <a href="/nativescript-mason/guide/scrolling">Scrolling</a>.</p><h2 id="browser-defaults" tabindex="-1">Browser defaults <a class="header-anchor" href="#browser-defaults" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Browser defaults&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Elements come with browser-like user-agent defaults: heading sizes and margins, paragraph margins, list indentation and markers, <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> whitespace, and so on. They sit at the lowest priority, so any rule in your stylesheet overrides them. Turn them off globally with <a href="/nativescript-mason/guide/installation#preflight-optional">preflight</a>.</p><h2 id="where-masonkit-semantics-apply" tabindex="-1">Where MasonKit semantics apply <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-masonkit-semantics-apply" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where MasonKit semantics apply&quot;">​</a></h2><p>MasonKit&#39;s CSS semantics apply to MasonKit elements only. A plain NativeScript view keeps NativeScript&#39;s semantics even when it sits inside a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>, including NativeScript&#39;s meaning of <code>px</code> (a device pixel). Installing MasonKit does not change how the rest of your app lays out. See <a href="/nativescript-mason/styling/units">Units</a> and <a href="/nativescript-mason/guide/mixing-views">Mixing with NativeScript views</a>.</p>',25)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
